course: everyday
lesson: 23
title: Understanding cats
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Cats talk with their ears, tail, eyes and voice: a tail held straight up with a little hook at the tip is a friendly hello.
explain: A frightened cat puffs its tail up like a bottle brush and arches its back to look bigger; an annoyed cat flicks or thumps its tail and turns its ears back or flat.
explain: Slow blinks mean a cat feels safe with you, while wide, round pupils in good light can mean it is scared or excited.
explain: Purring usually means a cat is content, but cats also purr when they are hurt, ill or stressed, so you must watch the rest of the body too.
explain: Let a cat come to you, stroke it gently on its cheeks, chin and the base of its ears, and stop as soon as its tail starts to twitch, its skin ripples or its ears turn back.
explain: For example, a cat that rolls onto its back is showing it trusts you, but rubbing its belly often ends in a scratch, because most cats protect their tummy.
explain: A boy in New York grabbed a cat and held it tight while it struggled; it scratched his face deeply, and the scratch got infected.
explain: A girl in Glasgow ignored her cat's flicking tail and flattened ears and kept rubbing its tummy; it bit her hand, and her hand swelled up and she needed antibiotics.
fact: A tail held straight up is a cat's friendly greeting.
fact: A puffed-up tail and an arched back mean a cat is frightened.
fact: A tail that flicks, swishes or thumps usually means the cat is annoyed.
fact: Ears turned back or flattened mean a cat is scared or angry.
fact: A slow blink is a sign that a cat trusts you.
fact: Cats usually purr when content, but they can also purr when hurt or stressed.
fact: Most cats like to be stroked on the cheeks, under the chin and at the base of the ears.
fact: Many cats do not like their belly touched, even when they lie on their back.
fact: A hiss is a cat's warning to stay away.
fact: Stop stroking a cat when its tail twitches, its skin ripples or its ears turn back.
fact: Cat scratches and bites can get infected, so they should be washed with soap and water.
fact: Cat bites often get infected because their teeth are thin and go deep.
fact: A cat scratch or bite that becomes red, hot or swollen should be checked by a doctor.
exercise: What does a cat's tail held straight up usually mean? | A friendly hello | The cat is very angry | The cat is about to pounce | The cat is frightened
exercise: A cat's tail suddenly puffs up like a bottle brush. How does it feel? | Frightened | Sleepy | Very hungry | Happy
exercise: What does a cat's flicking, thumping tail usually tell you? | It is getting annoyed | It wants to play with you | It is very relaxed | It is about to fall asleep
exercise: Why does a frightened cat arch its back? | To look bigger to whatever scared it | To stretch after a long sleep in the sun | To show it wants food | To make its fur dry faster
exercise: A cat looks at you and blinks very slowly. What does it mean? | It feels safe and trusts you | It is about to scratch you hard | It has something in its eye | It wants you to go away
exercise: Does purring always mean a cat is happy? | No, cats can also purr when hurt or stressed | Yes, a cat only purrs when it is happy and calm | Yes, purring is how cats laugh | No, purring always means a cat is angry
exercise: Where do most cats like to be stroked? | On the cheeks, chin and base of the ears | On the tummy | On the tail | On the paws
exercise: A cat rolls onto its back. What is the safest thing to do? | Leave its belly alone and stroke its cheek if it likes | Rub its tummy hard | Pick it up by its front legs | Tickle its back paws
exercise: What does a hiss mean? | Stay away from me | Please stroke me | I am hungry | I am happy to see you
exercise: You are stroking a cat and its skin starts to ripple and its tail twitches. What should you do? | Stop stroking it and let it go | Stroke it faster to calm it | Hold it tighter so it stays still | Pull its tail gently to play
exercise: How should you start making friends with a new cat? | Sit quietly and let the cat come to you | Chase it round the house until it gets used to you | Pick it up straight away | Make loud noises to get its attention
exercise: A cat's ears are pressed flat against its head. How does it probably feel? | Scared or angry | Calm and sleepy | Curious and friendly | Hungry and bored
exercise: What should you do if a cat scratches you and breaks the skin? | Wash it with soap and water and tell an adult | Lick it clean and forget it | Cover it with mud | Shake your hand dry and ignore it, cat scratches are clean
test: Mittens walks up to Emma in her kitchen in Toronto with her tail straight up. What is Mittens saying? | Hello, I'm glad to see you | I'm angry, go away | I'm scared of you | I'm going to bite you
test: A cat in Perth sees a big dog. Its back arches and its fur stands on end. Why? | It is scared and trying to look bigger | It is happy and wants to play | It is stretching after a nap | It is warming its fur in the sun on purpose
test: Which is true? | A cat may purr even when it is hurt | Cats purr only when they are happy | Cats never use their tails to show feelings | A slow blink means a cat is angry
test: Jack strokes his cat on the head. Its tail begins to swish and its ears turn back. What has the cat decided? | It has had enough and wants him to stop | It wants more strokes, and harder ones too | It is falling asleep happily | It wants to be picked up
test: Why do many cats scratch when someone rubs their belly? | The belly is soft and cats like to protect it | Cats are ticklish and laugh with their claws instead | The belly is where cats feel hungry | Cats think the hand is a toy mouse
test: A cat hides under a bed with big round pupils and flat ears after fireworks. What should you do? | Leave it alone and let it calm down | Pull it out to cuddle it | Shine a torch in its eyes | Bang on the bed to make it come out
test: A cat at a friend's house in Belfast hisses at Grace. What should she do? | Step back and leave the cat alone | Hiss back so it learns who is boss | Reach under to stroke it at once | Pick it up to show it she is kind
test: Which sign tells you a cat feels safe with you? | It blinks slowly while looking at you | Its tail is puffed up | It hisses when you come close to it | Its ears are flat back
test: Why must a cat scratch be washed even if it is small? | Cat scratches can carry germs and get infected | Cat claws contain poison | It will turn into a big scar if you don't wash it | Small scratches never matter at all
test: cat's tail is straight up as she comes over | She's saying a friendly hello | She is about to attack | She is frightened | She is hungry and cross
test: cat's tail swishing n thumping while i stroke him | Stop, he's getting annoyed | Stroke harder | Stroke his belly instead | He's purring with his tail
test: cat rolled over, tummy rub? | Better not, many cats hate it | Yes, that's what she wants | Yes, cats love belly rubs | Yes, if she's purring
test: cat scratched me, bit of blood | Wash it with soap and water | Lick it clean | Put a plaster on dirty skin | Leave it, scratches never matter
test: cat's tail is lashing and ears flat but i wanna cuddle | Leave her alone, she is about to scratch or bite | Pick her up and squeeze her | Rub her tummy to calm her, cats love that when cross | Blow in her face to play
test: cat bit me and now my hand is red and puffy | Tell an adult, a cat bite may need a doctor | Wait a week and see | Squeeze it to get the bite out | Put butter on it
test: Ruby says cats only scratch when they're mean. right? | No, cats scratch when scared, hurt or handled too much | Yes, nice cats never scratch anyone, only mean ones do | Yes, only street cats scratch | Yes, unless they are kittens
test: Emily's nan says her cat purred while it was dying so cats purr when they're sad, every time | Not every time; cats usually purr when content but can also purr when hurt or stressed | She is right, purring always means sadness | She is right, cats only purr before dying | She is right, so purring cats must see a vet
test: someone said a cat scratched a boy's eye out when he rubbed its belly, it's going round school | Probably exaggerated, but many cats hate belly rubs, so do not touch the belly | It is true, so all cats are dangerous | It is made up, so belly rubs are fine | It proves cats only scratch boys
test: Daisy's family have 6 cats and people say her house stinks, is it ok to say | No, it is unkind gossip and may not even be true | Yes, because six cats proves it | Yes, so she cleans up her house | Yes, but only to her best friend
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a tail held vertically upright by a cat? | It constitutes a friendly greeting | It constitutes a warning to keep away | It indicates that the cat is frightened | It indicates that the cat is in pain
exam: Identify the meaning conveyed by a slow blink on the part of a cat. | That the cat trusts the person observed | That the cat is about to attack | That the cat suffers from an eye disease | That the cat is very hungry
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the cessation of stroking. | Stroking should cease when the tail twitches, the skin ripples or the ears turn back | Stroking should cease only when the cat purrs | Stroking should continue until the cat hisses | Stroking should cease only when the cat sleeps
exam: It is given that a cat's tail is puffed up and its back is arched. Determine the state of the animal. | The cat is frightened | The cat is content and relaxed | The cat is inviting play | The cat is about to sleep
